I wired H53 (balanced GEMM1 token-range tiles under the image counters) into tc_kernel. It wins clearly on one row: **16,384 runs 46.4 µs against 50.5 µs for r12-merge (-8.3 %)**. The full success gate is missed: 8,384 only ties, and the 7x7 rows are slower than their r12-merge paths. Take 16,384 from this kernel and nothing else.

**Results.** 16/16 pass, including the portal-order re-runs. The run_tests reference numbers were identical across both of my runs, so they look cached rather than re-measured. The in-container A/B against r12's exact tc_kernel is the comparison I trust; the two agree in direction.

| Row | This kernel | r12-merge (run_tests) | Same-container A/B vs r12 tc_kernel |
|---|---|---|---|
| 16,384 | 46.4 | 50.5 (tc2) | 46.1 vs 50.9 |
| 8,384 | 33.7 | 33.9 | 33.4 vs 33.8 |
| 16,768 | 40.9 | 40.8 | — |
| 8,768 | 32.5 | 30.6 | slower by ~1.8 |
| 32,768 | 57.7 | 54.7 | slower by ~4.5 |
| 2,768 | 24.8 | 22.7 | — |

The other 10 rows run the r0 fallback; this candidate is for the per-row merge only.

**Gate.** 16,384 meets its line (-4.1 µs, need ≤ -3). 8,384 misses (-0.2 µs, need -1.5), and no 7x7 row improves. The refutation clause does not fire.

**What I learned:**
- **The biggest finding is a cost in how a tile signals "done".** Using `fence.acq_rel.gpu` plus a relaxed add, instead of r12's `red.release.gpu`, cost 8–15 µs per call at 14x14 (8,384: 42.3 → 34.5; 16,384: 67.1 → 51.9), though in-kernel timestamps barely showed it. The shipped build uses `red.release`.
- **Balanced tiles only pay at 14x14.** On the 7x7 rows, tiles that straddle images tied or lost (32,768 with two tiles per CTA: 68.9 vs 61.2 µs), so 7x7 keeps whole-image tiles.
- **Straddling tiles carry a fixed cost.** Rebuilding the per-image GRN sum from partial sums costs 1.2–2.2 µs per tile, mostly memory latency.
- **Cache hints don't qualify.** Following the operator rule, I applied them only to our own workspace buffers. They saved at most 0.35 µs, under the 0.7 µs keep bar, so they are off.
- **Other GEMM2 splits don't help.** KS 3 stays best for both 14x14 rows.

**Correctness:**
- **Precision:** 10 rounds against an fp64 reference on all six rows gave 57–67 % of atol and 0 elements beyond it. The final build changes only the counter signalling, not the arithmetic.
- **Spills:** compile_b200 of the final device code shows 0 B local (128 / 109 registers).

**Not done:**
- **Unexplained 7x7 regression.** The 7x7 rows are still 2–3 µs slower than r12's kernel. I couldn't find the cause in the time available; it's recorded as an open item.
